## Findings
- SR-01 | medium | attribution | “0.6", 3.1", 23.75"” in the aperture-radius examples | The excerpts describe a “0.6" circular aperture” (2019NatAs...3..314L), “3.1″ circle” (2024ESC.....8..665M), and “23.75″ circle” (2024IJAsB..23...14M), without establishing radii. Only the AFGL 4176 and AGB examples explicitly specify radius. | Preserve the stated geometry; mark radius versus diameter unspecified where the pack does not resolve it.
- SR-02 | medium | attribution | Parameter-table “Papers (count)” values | Detection thresholds occur in at least nine on-topic entries, including 2025ApJS..276...49J, versus eight claimed. Numerical linewidth settings occur in at least eight papers, versus six; source-size/filling-factor settings likewise occur in at least eight, versus six. The latter two rows omit 2018A&A...616A..90C and/or 2024ESC.....8..665M, and 2019NatAs...3..314L and 2020ApJ...896...35J, respectively. | Recount using an explicit per-row bibliography and a consistent definition of a stated setting.
- SR-03 | medium | attribution | “Tex, hot cores/corinos … 90 to 300 K”; “FWHM … 1.1 to 5 km/s” | The hot-core row includes two V883 Ori disk studies. The pack gives hot-core temperatures up to 335 K in 2025A&A...694A.166C. Numerical linewidth examples include 0.8 km/s in 2018A&A...616A..90C and 5.2 km/s in 2024ESC.....8..665M. | Separate source classes and distinguish explored grids, fixed assumptions, and fitted results; correct the ranges or explicitly restrict their population.
- SR-04 | medium | attribution | “RADEX … non-LTE check … [2]” | The excerpts support one explicit RADEX non-LTE identification check: 2018A&A...615A..28D. The “LTE-RADEX module” in 2024ESC.....8..665M is explicitly used for LTE fitting, not a second non-LTE check. | Report one non-LTE RADEX example and retain the cyanamide module under CASSIS LTE fitting.
- SR-05 | medium | attribution | Whole-band LTE modelling examples, including “MADEX in 2013ApJ...778L..25C” | That excerpt establishes only “Line identification with MADEX.” Likewise, 2025A&A...694A.166C models HC3N states, rather than documenting a full-band model of all identified species. | Separate documented full-spectrum modelling from targeted LTE fitting and catalogue identification; limit each software attribution to its stated scope.
- SR-06 | medium | attribution | “Call it tentative when it rests on few lines … CO/OH in 2019A&A...623L...1K” | The pack’s limitation attributes the tentative CO/OH results to “simple methods” and says non-LTE modelling is needed; it does not attribute that status to few lines. The B335 card also does not establish why acetone is tentative. | State each paper’s documented reason; use FeO as the explicitly supported single-transition example.
- SR-07 | low | attribution | “250 +/- 25 K” in parameters.md | The 2024ESC.....8..665M result explicitly flags ±25 K in the abstract versus ±35 K in Section 3.2; its settings card leaves the discrepancy unresolved. | Include both reported uncertainties and flag the inconsistency.
- SR-08 | medium | unsupported | “Lines needed for detection … 3 per state to 25 per species” | In 2025A&A...694A.166C, three lines per state are required **to fit**. The 25/12/14 values in 2020A&A...644A..84M are selected line counts, not detection minima. | Rename the row “Lines used in these analyses” and separate fitting requirements from detection evidence.
- SR-09 | medium | science | “Velocity window for a match … sigma floored at channel width” | The 2019A&A...623L...1K setting reports a velocity uncertainty and a sigma floor; the pack does not establish that either defines a catalogue-matching acceptance window. | Separate centroid uncertainty, profile width, and matching tolerance. Require an explicit tolerance incorporating spectral resolution and relevant frequency/velocity uncertainties.
- SR-10 | medium | science | “Blend rule … Range seen: tau < 0.2” | Optical depth is not a blending criterion. In 2018A&A...616A..90C, blended and optically thick lines are excluded separately; tau < 0.2 concerns opacity. | Give blending and optical-depth selection separate rows and independent flags.
- SR-11 | high | science | “The same LTE parameters … including … isotopologues” | Read literally, this also ties column density across isotopologues, imposing equal columns. The pack instead documents an explicit CH3OH/13CH3OH column scaling of 60 in 2019NatAs...3..314L; 2024AJ....167...66Y specifies common size, Tex and linewidth. | Explicitly allow separate isotopologue columns or justified isotope ratios; name which other parameters are tied and why.
- SR-12 | medium | science | “When two lines overlap, fit them jointly with shared width and velocity” | Shared kinematics are a source-specific assumption in 2018ApJ...866L..12G, not a general consequence of overlap. Its model also assumes optically thin LTE CN at 300 K and includes a constant continuum. | Make shared kinematics conditional on evidence for a common component; compare alternative constraints and propagate deblending uncertainty.
- SR-13 | medium | science | “For weak disk lines, filter instead of aperture-averaging” | This occurs in the spectrum-building step, but 2020ApJ...893..101L produces a **normalized filter impulse response**, not a calibrated brightness spectrum for LTE fitting. | Separate filter-based detection from calibrated flux/spectrum extraction; prohibit treating response significance as line brightness.
- SR-14 | medium | omission | Hyperfine components “scaled by catalogue line strengths” | The 2017A&A...598A..53D card makes optically thin LTE an assumption and explicitly warns that it is likely invalid. The skill imports the component-scaling method without that warning. | State its assumptions and require an opacity/non-LTE assessment before adopting fixed component ratios.
- SR-15 | medium | omission | Reporting and verification requirements omit an uncertainty procedure | The pack provides N/Tex perturbation checks (2018A&A...616A..90C), calibration plus fitting errors (2019NatAs...3..314L), Monte Carlo fit-error checks (2020ApJ...896...35J), and an AA Tex–N degeneracy causing approximately factor-two uncertainty (2020A&A...644A..84M). These are absent from the actionable reporting requirements. | Require the uncertainty method, calibration treatment, parameter degeneracies, and sensitivity to fixed assumptions to accompany the checking model.
- SR-16 | medium | usability | “100%” matching; “P and D factor tests”; “three explicit selection rules” | The screening card flags isolated-line criteria and matching tolerance as unstated (2025ApJS..276...49J). The supplied excerpts do not define P/D acceptance thresholds or enumerate all three GUAPOS rules. These instructions cannot be reproduced from the skill. | Supply verified definitions and thresholds where available; otherwise mark them unavailable and require consultation of the original method before execution.

## Notes
The core workflow and most individual numerical examples are well supported.
The main risks are converting study-specific assumptions into defaults and conflating detection, fitting, blending and opacity criteria.
Keep pack-reported practice clearly separate from additional recommendations.
